# CosmosJourneyer TLS operations

This document describes the TLS renewal contract for {{ production_server_names | join(' and ') }}. The source of
truth is the production VPS configuration in `infra/production-vps`.

## Invariants

1. The ACME webroot is permanent and independent from application releases: `{{ acme_webroot_path }}`.
2. Certbot records that exact webroot in `/etc/letsencrypt/renewal/{{ production_domain }}.conf`.
3. Port 80 serves `/.well-known/acme-challenge/` directly from that webroot before the HTTPS redirect. The redirect must
   remain inside `location /`; a server-level `return` intercepts ACME challenges.
4. The webroot must never move under an application release or `current` symlink.
5. Certbot owns all certificate material under `/etc/letsencrypt`; Ansible never templates private keys or certificates.
6. A successful renewal runs `/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/reload-nginx`, which validates nginx before reload.

## Initial issuance

DNS for every production name must already point to this VPS. Bootstrap the certificate explicitly:

```sh
ansible-playbook site.yml --tags tls -e tls_bootstrap_certificate=true
```

Normal playbook executions never request a new certificate when one is missing.

## Checks

Validate nginx and simulate renewal without Certbot's random sleep:

```sh
nginx -t
certbot renew --dry-run --no-random-sleep-on-renew
```

Before a renewal test, place a temporary token under `{{ acme_webroot_path }}/.well-known/acme-challenge/` and confirm
both production names return it over plain HTTP without a redirect or 404. Remove the token after the test.

Inspect the certificate actually served:

```sh
openssl s_client -connect {{ production_domain }}:443 -servername {{ production_domain }} </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
  | openssl x509 -noout -subject -issuer -dates -serial
```

## Monitoring and alerting

Certbot renewal failures trigger `cosmos-tls-alert@snap.certbot.renew.service`. The daily
`cosmos-tls-check.timer` compares the local and publicly served certificates and fails when either is invalid or expires
within {{ tls_expiry_threshold_days }} days. Failures send an email to {{ tls_contact_email }}.

The notifier sends directly to `{{ tls_smtp_host }}` over STARTTLS because the VPS has no local mail transfer agent.

Useful commands:

```sh
systemctl status cosmos-tls-check.timer
journalctl -u cosmos-tls-check.service
journalctl -u snap.certbot.renew.service
systemctl list-dependencies snap.certbot.renew.service
```

## Incident recovery

1. Confirm DNS still points every production name to this VPS.
2. Confirm the HTTP ACME challenge path is publicly reachable.
3. Run the simulated renewal.
4. Run the real renewal only if the certificate is expired or near expiry.
5. Confirm the public certificate serial and dates.
6. Inspect Certbot and nginx journals before changing paths.
